# rav1d-safe vs the C reference (dav1d 1.5.4): the "near rav1d performance" yardstick

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: stable, release profile (lto = "fat", codegen-units = 1), default
       features (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16 — the CHECKED DisjointMut build).
       No `-C target-cpu=native`.
rav1d-safe commit: a6a7e23 (main — #445 as merged; NOT the open #450 branch)
C reference:       dav1d 1.5.4, Homebrew, /opt/homebrew/bin/dav1d

## nice level — read this before reproducing

NO `nice` ON ANY TIMED RUN. On Darwin a positive nice value maps the process to
background QoS (efficiency cores) and distorts wall clock by well over an order
of magnitude. `nice -n 19` was used for BUILDS only.

## Which C reference, and why not the `rav1d` crate

Three candidates were checked:

1. `dav1d` 1.5.4 CLI + `libdav1d.7.dylib` (Homebrew) — AVAILABLE, and used here.
   This is the C original that rav1d (and therefore rav1d-safe) is a port of, so
   it is the right yardstick, not a proxy for one.

2. The `rav1d` crate 1.1.0 from crates.io — CANNOT BE BUILT on aarch64 with its
   default features. The failure is NOT Apple `cc` rejecting the `.S` syntax (a
   bare `cc -march=armv8.6-a+sve2 -c` on an empty assembler input succeeds). It
   is a packaging gap: `src/arm/64/refmvs.S` line 28 does
   `#include "src/arm/asm-offsets.h"`, and that header is neither shipped in the
   published tarball nor generated by the crate's `build.rs` (which only writes
   `config.asm`/`config.h` into `OUT_DIR`). Upstream dav1d generates it from the
   meson build; the crate does not carry that step. Verbatim:

     src/arm/64/refmvs.S:28:10: fatal error: 'src/arm/asm-offsets.h' file not found

   With `--no-default-features --features bitdepth_8,bitdepth_16` it builds
   cleanly — but that is the no-asm pure-Rust path, which is not a performance
   reference for anything. Not measured.

3. `aomdec` / `avifdec` are present but are libaom, a different decoder lineage
   and much slower; they would not answer "near rav1d".

## Method

Vectors: the seven from `tile_threading_guard_2026-08-06.meta`, reused unchanged
(all-intra AVIF stills derived from `test-vectors/bench/photo_4k.avif`, 4:4:4,
q70; `v4k_8tile*` are `--tilecolslog2 2 --tilerowslog2 1` = 8 tiles, every other
vector is single-tile).

Both decoders are fed the SAME AV1 bitstream. `examples/dump_obu.rs` extracts the
AVIF primary item's OBU stream and also wraps N copies of it as an IVF, so dav1d
can be timed on repeated decodes of the identical frame that `bench_ab_decode`
loops over in-process.

  rs      `examples/bench_ab_decode` — managed API, N iterations in one process,
          3 timed reps per invocation.
  dav1d1  `dav1d --framedelay 1` — TILE THREADING ONLY. This is the
          apples-to-apples arm: the checked rav1d-safe build hard-codes
          `n_fc = 1` (src/lib.rs:126 — frame threading requires `unchecked`), so
          `--framedelay 1` puts dav1d on the same threading model.
  dav1dA  `dav1d` with default framedelay (frame threading allowed).

dav1d per-frame times come from `--frametimes`, so process startup and file IO
are excluded; the first 3 frames of every run are dropped as warmup.

Interleaving: all three arms run back to back within a (vector, threads) cell,
and the arm order is ROTATED each round. Medians are over 3 rounds x 3 reps
(n = 9) for `rs`, and over every post-warmup frame of every round for dav1d.

Contention control: this box was shared with a concurrent agent whose cargo
builds spiked to 3x100% CPU without warning. A pre-cell quiet check is not
enough — a build that starts mid-cell silently invalidates it. So the whole
cell is staged and only COMMITTED if the box was quiet before the cell and
after every arm in it; otherwise it is discarded and retried. Final run:
84/84 cells committed, 3 attempts discarded and retried, max 1-minute load
average 3.49.

Correctness: dav1d's `--muxer md5` and `bench_ab_decode`'s own plane md5 agree
exactly on v1024 (`0a17a5ebd63934a1c251b90858284f67`), so the two decoders are
producing bit-identical output on this content and the comparison is of equal
work. Every rav1d-safe checksum is single-valued across all four thread counts.

Commands:

  target/release/examples/dump_obu <in.avif> <out.obu> <out.ivf> <n> <w> <h>
  scripts/perf/yardstick_sweep.sh benchmarks/out.tsv 3
  scripts/perf/tier_sweep.sh benchmarks/tier.tsv 3

## Result 1 — ms/frame and the ratio to the C reference

Median ms/frame. `rs/dav1d1` is the headline: how many times slower rav1d-safe
is than dav1d at the same threading model.

  vector          geom              t        rs   dav1d1   rs/dav1d1
  v256            256x144   8bpc    1     1.197    0.770        1.56
  v256                             2     1.310    0.783        1.67
  v256                             4     1.302    0.778        1.67
  v256                             8     1.301    0.779        1.67
  v1024           1024x576  8bpc    1    52.951   15.431        3.43
  v1024                            2    54.647   14.331        3.81
  v1024                            4    54.499   14.254        3.82
  v1024                            8    54.660   14.262        3.83
  v1024_10b       1024x576 10bpc    1    54.325   15.578        3.49
  v1024_10b                        2    53.730   14.568        3.69
  v1024_10b                        4    53.732   14.506        3.70
  v1024_10b                        8    53.785   14.494        3.71
  v4k_1tile       3840x2160 8bpc    1   597.506  246.514        2.42
  v4k_1tile                        2   585.918  238.825        2.45
  v4k_1tile                        4   587.001  238.303        2.46
  v4k_1tile                        8   587.123  238.918        2.46
  v4k_1tile_10b   3840x2160 10bpc   1   666.046  252.218        2.64
  v4k_1tile_10b                    2   644.174  241.144        2.67
  v4k_1tile_10b                    4   644.601  240.777        2.68
  v4k_1tile_10b                    8   644.651  240.792        2.68
  v4k_8tile       3840x2160 8bpc    1   589.877  246.472        2.39
  v4k_8tile                        2   495.445  125.164        3.96
  v4k_8tile                        4   700.583   65.621       10.68
  v4k_8tile                        8  1157.718   34.321       33.73
  v4k_8tile_10b   3840x2160 10bpc   1   666.183  251.743        2.65
  v4k_8tile_10b                    2   547.335  127.765        4.28
  v4k_8tile_10b                    4   711.659   66.828       10.65
  v4k_8tile_10b                    8  1252.293   35.706       35.07

**The single-thread gap is 2.4x-3.5x** (1.56x on the 256x144 vector, where
per-frame fixed cost dominates both decoders). That is the honest "how near
rav1d are we" number, and it is not close.

**The multi-thread gap is up to 35x**, and all of that growth is the scaling
defect, not the kernels — see Result 2.

## Result 2 — dav1d's TILE threading scales nearly linearly; ours inverts

Speedup vs each arm's own t=1, on the 8-tile vectors. dav1d1 here is tile
threading only, the same thing rav1d-safe is doing, on the same bitstream.

  vector          arm        t=1   t=2   t=4   t=8
  v4k_8tile       rs        1.00  1.19  0.84  0.51
  v4k_8tile       dav1d1    1.00  1.97  3.76  7.18
  v4k_8tile_10b   rs        1.00  1.22  0.94  0.53
  v4k_8tile_10b   dav1d1    1.00  1.97  3.77  7.05

dav1d gets **7.18x on 8 threads across 8 tiles** — near-perfect. rav1d-safe
peaks at 1.19x on 2 threads and then goes backwards, ending at 0.51x, i.e.
2x SLOWER than single-threaded. This reproduces Result 5 of
`tile_threading_guard_2026-08-06.meta` independently, and adds the thing that
was missing: the same hardware, the same tiles, the same bitstream, with a
decoder that scales. The anti-scaling is not a property of the content, of
macOS, of the P/E-core split, or of 8 tiles being too few. It is ours.

On single-tile vectors NEITHER decoder scales (rs 0.97-1.03x, dav1d1
0.98-1.08x), which is the expected result and confirms the harness is not
inventing parallelism.

## Result 3 — the `dav1dA` (frame threading) column is NOT a throughput number

Recorded for completeness and flagged so nobody quotes it. On single-tile
content `--frametimes` under frame threading reported up to 512x speedup at
t=4 (v4k_1tile: 246.7 ms at t=1 vs 1.146 ms at t=4). That is impossible on 12
cores. The cause is the instrument, not the decoder: with a full pipeline the
CLI drains queued pictures in a burst, so the inter-output interval collapses to
queue-pop time while the real work already happened in parallel.

Where it can be cross-checked it agrees: on the 8-tile vectors dav1dA and
dav1d1 land within 2-7% of each other at every thread count (t=8: 32.0 vs
34.3 ms), because there the parallelism is tiles either way.

A wall-clock two-point fit (`total = alpha + beta*frames`) is the correct
instrument for the frame-threaded arm. It is written
(`scripts/perf/dav1d_wall.sh`) but NOT RUN — see "Not measured" below.

## Result 4 — the SIMD tier spread is ZERO on aarch64, for BOTH decoders

threads=1, median of 3 rounds:

  vector        rs_native  rs_scalar  scal/nat   dav1d_neon  dav1d_scal  s/n
  v1024            52.873     52.481      0.99       15.552      15.523  1.00
  v1024_10b        54.391     54.635      1.00       15.637      15.724  1.01
  v4k_1tile       595.910    592.617      0.99      246.879     247.160  1.00

Neither `Settings::cpu_level = CpuLevel::Scalar` nor dav1d's `--cpumask 0`
changes anything measurable. This is correct behaviour, not a broken harness,
and it is verified in source on our side: on aarch64 the only consumers of
`rav1d_set_cpu_flags_mask` are `summon_arm64v2`/`summon_arm64v3` (src/cpu.rs
~line 310), which are reachable only from the `rav1d_arm_dotprod`/
`rav1d_arm_i8mm` cfg-gated MC dispatch — both OFF by default. Everything else
goes through `Arm64::summon()`, which is `NeonToken` and can never be absent
because AArch64 mandates NEON. dav1d does the same thing for the same reason.

This corroborates `neon_tier_isolation_2026-07-28.meta` from a second
direction, and it has a consequence for the optimisation program:

**the scalar-vs-SIMD spread CANNOT be used to bound how much of the 2.4-3.5x
gap is kernels versus tracker on this machine.** There is no scalar tier to
measure against. That bound has to come from a tracker-off build instead
(`--features unchecked`, or the `__probe_untracked` / `__probe_noscan` /
`__probe_lockonly` features already present in rav1d-disjoint-mut).

## Not measured — and why

* **`--features unchecked` vs the checked build.** Both arms were built
  (`bench_ab_decode` patched to pin `max_frame_delay = 1` so an `unchecked`
  build does not silently gain frame threading, since `n_fc = 1` is forced only
  in the checked build) and the sweep script is committed as
  `scripts/perf/unchk_sweep.sh`. It was NOT RUN: a concurrent agent on this
  shared box started its own timed sweep over exactly this decomposition
  (`base`, `base2`, `lockonly`, `noscan`, `untracked`, `mainpure`,
  `probe_tracker` arms). Running mine would have corrupted theirs and duplicated
  the work. No number is reported for it.
* **dav1d frame-threaded throughput by wall clock** (`scripts/perf/dav1d_wall.sh`)
  — same reason, same box.
* **The `rav1d` crate as a reference** — cannot be built here, see above. No
  ratio against it is reported, estimated, or extrapolated.

